          "context": "The real personal-memory plugin loses Latin tokens embedded next to CJK: a memory containing DeepSeek\u63d2\u4ef6 cannot be found by querying deepseek. Its tokenizer also creates artificial CJK bigrams across punctuation, whitespace, and intervening non-CJK text. Fix both behaviors while preserving NFKC normalization, lowercase, deduplication, sorted output, and existing non-CJK word token behavior. Existing persistent v6 memory_tokens must also be upgraded transactionally and idempotently when reopened: reindex active records using content plus memoryKnowledgeText(knowledge), without reviving removed records, changing identity/version/audit data, or weakening namespace isolation. Owner design decision: this is an index algorithm migration with no table layout change. Keep DB schema version 6 and track the tokenizer-index version independently in existing schema_meta. Rebuild only once per index version inside the startup migration transaction, then persist the index-version marker. Add regression tests and concise README documentation. Read the file manifest first, then batch src/tokenize.ts, src/sqlite.ts, src/knowledge.ts, README.md and tests/retrieval.spec.ts in ONE read call. Existing test files need no modifications: add a new focused regression file under tests/. Propose complete replacements for read files and complete contents for added files. src/store.ts is over the source tool per-file bound; its current #replaceTokens deletes old tokens and inserts tokenizeMemory(content), with callers supplying `${content}\\n${memoryKnowledgeText(knowledge)}`; search uses tokenizeMemory(query), intersects stored memory_tokens before phrase ranking. Preserve those call sites. Existing migration tests assert schema 6, which remains valid because the table layout is unchanged. Keep all changes inside plugins/personal-memory. Prepare one durable pending modification. Do not claim owner approval, release, activation or successful independent evaluation